An exploratory analysis of historical water-quality records identified a localized cluster of elevated nitrate concentrations near Buena Vista–Talbot Roads within the Middle Willamette watershed (HUC-8 17090007).

Four nearby monitoring locations recorded 15 dissolved nitrate observations between November 1994 and August 1995. Thirteen observations (86.7%) exceeded the exploratory threshold of 8 mg/L as nitrogen. All four locations recorded at least one elevated measurement, while three recorded elevated results in a majority of their observations.

The cluster included a stream, a hyporheic-zone well, and two groundwater wells. Elevated measurements occurred across all three monitoring environments. On March 30, 1995, three locations representing both surface water and groundwater recorded concentrations between 9.99 and 16 mg/L as nitrogen.

The surrounding landscape was predominantly agricultural in the land-cover layer examined. This provides a plausible context for investigating agricultural nutrient inputs and groundwater–surface-water interactions, but does not establish an agricultural source or causal relationship.

All 15 observations used USGS parameter code 618 and carried a Historical result-status designation. Comparability of parameter coding supports the exploratory comparison, although consistency of laboratory methods and sampling procedures has not yet been established.

Limitations: The analysis is based on a small number of historical observations and monitoring locations. Sampling frequency varies, the observations are not necessarily statistically independent, and the land-cover information examined may not represent conditions during 1994–1995. The 8 mg/L threshold is an exploratory analytical cutoff, not a regulatory determination.

Preliminary interpretation: The Buena Vista–Talbot area warrants further investigation as a potential historical nitrate hotspot exhibiting elevated concentrations across multiple aquatic environments.
